## 1. Git and GitHub fundamentals

### Q1. What is Git?

Spoken answer:

Git is a distributed version control system. We use Git to track changes in our source code and manage different versions of our application.

In my project, multiple developers work on the same .NET 8 application. Each developer makes code changes and commits them using Git.

This helps us track who made the changes, when the changes were made, and what exactly was modified.

It also helps us go back to previous versions if something goes wrong.

### Q2. What is GitHub?

GitHub is a cloud-based platform where we host and manage Git repositories.

In our project, we use GitHub to store our application source code.

Developers push their code into GitHub, create feature branches, raise pull requests, and participate in code reviews.

We also integrate GitHub with Jenkins to trigger our CI/CD pipeline whenever new code is pushed.

### Q3. What is the difference between Git and GitHub?

| Git | GitHub |
| --- | --- |
| Version control system | Cloud-based code hosting platform |
| Works locally | Hosts repositories remotely |
| Tracks code changes | Supports collaboration |
| Uses commands like commit and merge | Provides pull requests and code reviews |
| Can work without internet | Requires network access for remote operations |

Spoken answer:

Git is the tool we use to manage source code versions, whereas GitHub is the platform where we host our Git repositories and collaborate with other developers.

### Q4. What is a Git repository?

A Git repository is a location where Git tracks our project files, commits, branches, and history.

We have two types:

- Local repository – Available on the developer's machine.
- Remote repository – Hosted on GitHub.

### Q5. What is the difference between Git and SVN?

Git is a distributed version control system, whereas SVN is a centralized version control system.

In Git, every developer has a local copy of the repository and its history.

In SVN, developers generally depend on a central repository for version history and operations.

Git supports easier branching and merging, which makes it very useful for modern development teams.

